"""Adds to the note beyond up-sets a dated section on the Lean proof of the lemma on lifts, on the prime 2 and on the Lean proofs
for it, as it stands after the referee's two readings of the earlier forms (apply_addendum.py, apply_addendum2.py); one mark in
'In short' where a sentence is overtaken; one line in the list of files.
usage: make_beyond2.py read-text-beyond.txt | apply_addendum3.py ADDED-AT READ-FILE NOON-FILE LEANTWO-FILE > beyond-up-sets.md"""
import sys
s = sys.stdin.read(); when = sys.argv[1]; read = open(sys.argv[2]).read().strip(); noon = open(sys.argv[3]).read().strip(); leantwo = open(sys.argv[4]).read().strip()
def rep(a, b):
    global s
    assert s.count(a) == 1, (s.count(a), a[:70]); s = s.replace(a, b)
rep("when that prime is 2 it is not proved here.", "when that prime is 2 it is not proved here. [Added 26 November: since proved, on paper and then in Lean; section 7.]")
sec = f"""
## 7. Added {when}: the lemma on lifts in Lean, the prime 2, and what they settle

*{read} Three sentences above were written before this section and are overtaken by it: in "In short", "when that prime is 2 it is not proved here" (it now is); in section 2, "For p = 2 … I have no argument" (there is now one for the lifts, and still none for the translates); and the first bullet of section 6 (the same).*

The lemma on lifts of section 2 is now proved in Lean. I fixed the statements before any Lean proof of them; a fresh instance wrote the proofs; another audited the statements and found no difference from what is meant. The files are in the shared library (`../../../lean-tiling/Pnq/Lift.lean`, `Lift1.lean`, `LiftMin.lean`), added after Noon had read the statements; {noon} Copies, my checks and the audit are in `lean-lift/` here. The lemma, with a proof in a few lines by counting roots (it does not need the degree of a field, as the reader's proof above does, and it is the proof that the Lean files follow), is written out in the paper, `../../../paper/minsize.pdf`, section "Larger groups". Until the referee of this section asked, nobody had searched for the lemma in print. I have now made a short search myself, seven requests, for it and for the lemma on signs below, and found neither (`lean-lift/search-in-print.md`, with the nearest things found); that is weak evidence, and lemmas as simple as these may well be known.

**What Lean certifies for an odd prime** (`lean-lift/Lift.lean`, `lean-lift/LiftMin.lean`; r is a prime, and odd where it says so):

- `sum_reduce` and `cyclotomic_dvd_reduce_iff`: reducing a multiset on Z_{{N·r}} modulo N keeps its size and, for every d dividing N, whether Φ_d divides its mask (for every prime r, 2 included).
- `exists_lift`: for r odd, every nonempty multiset on Z_N is the reduction of a multiset on Z_{{N·r}} whose mask is divisible by no Φ_d with d dividing N·r and not N. This is the first lemma of section 2 in the form "some lift will do"; the particular lift is in the Lean proof (`Aux.exists_lift_aux`, `Aux.not_cyclotomic_dvd_of_single`), which are the proving instance's lemmas and were not among the statements fixed or audited.
- `sizes_eq`: for r odd and a set P of divisors of N, the sizes of the nonempty multisets with exactly the pattern P are the same on Z_N and on Z_{{N·r}}.
- `minsize_unbalanced_lift`, `minsize_balanced_all_lift`, `minsize_balanced_lift`: the three statements of the theorem for Z_{{pⁿq}}, on Z_{{pⁿq·r}} with r an odd prime, for the multisets whose pattern is made of divisors of pⁿq and has no other divisor of pⁿqr.

The reduction used for r₁ and r₂ in section 1 is thus in Lean for one prime step, 2 included (`sum_reduce`, `cyclotomic_dvd_reduce_iff`).

**The prime 2.** For r = 2 the particular lift of section 2 does fail (3 of 200 random multisets there), as it may: the proof uses 2·r^k ≤ (r − 1)·r^k. What is needed is less, that some lift has no new divisor, and for the groups of this note that is now proved, first on paper and then in Lean. It comes in two steps.

*From lifts to signs (mine).* Write N = 2^k·N′ with N′ odd. An element of Z_N has two lifts to Z_{{2N}}, a low one and a high one (their residues modulo 2^(k+1) differ by 2^k). Give every element a sign, + for low and − for high. For d′ dividing N′, the polynomial Φ_{{d′·2^(k+1)}} divides the mask of the lift exactly when, for every class modulo 2^k, the signed mask of the elements in that class, taken modulo N′, is divisible by Φ_{{d′}}; this is the count of roots again, with 2^k roots and a polynomial of degree below 2^k. So it is enough to sign the elements of one nonempty class so that their signed mask on Z_{{N′}} is divisible by no Φ_{{d′}}, d′ dividing N′ (d′ = 1 included: the signs must not add up to 0), and to lift the other classes anyhow. For N odd there is one class, and lifting along 2 and signing are the same question.

*The lemma on signs (not mine).* I put that question, with what I knew, to a fresh instance, to prove or refute (`lean-lift/brief-sign-lemma.md`). It proved it for n = p^a·q^b, with p and q odd primes and a, b ≥ 0, and for n = p·r·q^b with three different odd primes; together these cover every odd n below 1155 = 3·5·7·11. Its text is `lean-lift/sign-lemma-result.md`, its programs `lean-lift/sign/`. In the first case the signs are chosen class by class modulo the powers of p, along the path from one element's class up to the whole group, so that modulo q the sum of all signs is not 0 and at each level two classes with the same parent have different sums; that this is enough follows from Φ_{{p^i·q^j}} ≡ Φ_{{p^i}} to a power, modulo q. The second case uses a signing of a rectangle of cells in which every row sum and column sum is 0 or ±1. By the instance's account (its §7) a further instance, which it asked, read its proofs and reported no error; no report of that reading is on file, and I have only the instance's word for it. I have read both proofs and found no error, and the first case is written out, in a form of mine that needs one lemma less, in the paper's section "Larger groups" (Lemmas 17 and 18 there). For general odd n the lemma on signs is open, the instance says exactly where (its §6); the stronger form I had asked about, that all signs + or one sign turned is always enough, is neither proved nor refuted.

*Tested.* The lift along 2 built in this way, in an implementation of my own from the description (`lean-lift/two/lift_two.py`): on 1,400 multisets on 28 groups Z_N, N up to 150 with odd part of the first form, the lift to Z_{{2N}} had exactly the pattern of the multiset in all 1,400; the plain lift, every element low, had it in 1,117. The searches made before the proof agree with it, and all of them lie where it reaches (odd parts below 1155): the audit found a lift with no new divisor for every multiset it tried with r = 2 (by going through 2,530,899 multisets on the larger group in fifteen cases, N up to 30, and for 1,618,904 multisets on Z_N in eighteen cases, N up to 105). For the stronger form, which is not proved, all signs + or one sign turned was enough in my own searches, for every subset of Z_9, Z_15 and Z_21 that contains 0 and for 279,961 random subsets of Z_n for eight values of n up to 315 (the case of multisets reduces to sets; `lean-lift/sign-mine/`). Where nothing is proved, in Z_1155, Z_1575 and Z_3465, the instance found all signs + or one sign turned enough in 5,998, 5,998 and 2,999 random samples.

*In Lean* (`lean-lift/two-lean/`: `Sign.lean`, `Lift2.lean`, `LiftAll.lean`, 1,153 lines). I fixed eight statements, and posted a hash of them on the board before any proof was begun; three fresh instances wrote the proofs at the same time, one file each. What they certify:

- `exists_signs`: the lemma on signs for n = p^a·q^b (a signing is a splitting of the multiset into the elements signed + and those signed −).
- `exists_lift_two` and `sizes_eq_two`: the lift along 2, and the equality of sizes, when N = 2^k·p^a·q^b. (Inside the proof, a lemma of the proving instance, `TwoAux.exists_lift_two_aux`, is my reduction for every odd N′: if the lemma on signs holds on Z_{{N′}}, the lift along 2 exists on Z_{{2^k·N′}}. It was not among the statements fixed.)
- `sizes_eq_odd`: for every N and odd s, the sizes of the nonempty multisets with exactly a pattern of divisors of N are the same on Z_N and on Z_{{N·s}}.
- `sizes_eq_mul`, `minsize_unbalanced_mul`, `minsize_balanced_all_mul`, `minsize_balanced_mul`: the same for Z_{{pⁿq}} and Z_{{pⁿq·s}} with every s ≥ 1, and the three statements of the theorem for Z_{{pⁿq}} on Z_{{pⁿq·s}}, for the multisets whose pattern is made of divisors of pⁿq and has no other divisor of pⁿq·s.

{leantwo}

Not in Lean: the lemma on signs for n = p·r·q^b, the second lemma of section 2 (translates), the five moves beyond lifting, LB, nor anything else that is new in this note (what Lean has of the earlier theorems is said in section 1).

**What that settles here.** The theorem for Z_{{pⁿq}} holds as it stands on Z_{{pⁿq·s}} for every s ≥ 1, for the patterns made of divisors of pⁿq: lift along the factors 2 of s first (the odd part of the group's order is then that of pⁿq, which has the first form), then along the odd primes, for which the lemma holds on every group. On Z_{{p²q²}} this gives the patterns among the divisors of p²q (with r = q the group Z_{{p²q·q}} is Z_{{p²q²}}) and those among the divisors of pq², for all p and q. So the 55 patterns of "In short", for which that theorem gave only a lower bound, are now theorems on all three groups: on Z_225 all 55 by a lift along an odd prime; on Z_36 and on Z_100 the 31 that lie inside 12 (inside 20) by the lift along 3 (along 5), and the other 24, inside 18 (inside 50) and not inside 12 (20), by the lift along 2. (When r = p the lifted statement is the old theorem one level up, as Noon pointed out; the new ground is r = q and a third prime.)
"""
a = "\n## Files\n"
assert s.count(a) == 1
s = s.replace(a, sec + a)
rep("`either.py` (the first tally, with three moves) ·", "`either.py` (the first tally, with three moves) · section 7: `lean-lift/` (the three Lean files for an odd prime, the statements as fixed, my checks, the audit, the brief; `two-lean/`, the same for the prime 2 and every s; `brief-sign-lemma.md`, `sign-lemma-result.md` and `sign/`, the lemma on signs by the instance that proved it; `sign-mine/` and `two/`, my searches and my test of the lift along 2; `search-in-print.md`; `referee-lift/`, the referee's three reports on this section and its programs) ·")
sys.stdout.write(s)
